-- title:
--   Reducedness of D[X]/(g) for g monic and separable over Frac D
-- statement:
--   Let $D$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain, and let $g \in D[X]$ be a monic polynomial. Assume that the image of $g$ in $K[X]$, where $K$ is the fraction field `FractionRing D` of $D$ and the map is coefficientwise application of the structure map $D \to K$, is a separable polynomial, that is, that it is coprime to its derivative in $K[X]$. The conclusion is that the quotient ring $D[X]/(g)$, with $(g)$ the ideal generated by the single element $g$, is reduced: its only nilpotent element is $0$. No hypothesis of separability or of monicity is imposed on $g$ beyond these; in particular $g$ is not required to be nonconstant, and the case $g = 1$, where the quotient is the zero ring, is covered.
--
--   An elementary reducedness criterion: divisibility by a monic polynomial over a domain can be tested after passing to the fraction field, and separable polynomials over a field are squarefree. It is used in the project to see that algebras of the form 'adjoin a root of a separable polynomial' remain reduced after the base changes occurring in the analysis of modular curves of level $p$, being cited by [`Subalgebra.isReduced_tensor_of_separable`](thm.html#Subalgebra.isReduced_tensor_of_separable) and by [`ModularCurve.LevelP.quotientByLine_eq_of_inLine`](thm.html#ModularCurve.LevelP.quotientByLine_eq_of_inLine).
